    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d450">

                <pb facs="0536" n="524" sameAs="frus:frus1942v01/pg524"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.6354/367: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-06-15">June 15, 1942</date>.<lb/>[Received June 15—3:47
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3315. Embassy’s 1924, April 17, 3142, June 5,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d450-f1"/>
                    Department’s 2674, June 11.</p>
                <p>(1) At meeting International Tin Committee today representatives of Malayan,
                    Nigerian, Dutch and Belgian Congo Governments agreed to renewal International
                    Tin Agreement as of January 1st 1942; if necessary without Bolivian assent. No
                    communiqué will be issued.</p>
                <p>Your representative confined his statement to instruction given in second section
                    of Department’s 2674.</p>
                <p>(2) At opening of meeting Pearce, representative of Bolivia, informed Committee
                    that he had not received any instruction from the Bolivian Government and
                    therefore was not in a position to make any statement as to Bolivian attitude
                    concerning renewal.</p>
                <p>Towards close of meeting Chargé d’Affaires of Bolivian Legation here personally
                    appeared at Committee room and delivered to chairman a telegraphic message—which
                    he said had just been received—stating that Legation would be authorized to sign
                    new tin agreement when new price arrangements with United States of America have
                    been formally concluded.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d450-f2"/></p>
                <p>(3) When new tin agreement is formally signed<anchor n="14" xml:id="d450-f3"/> it
                    will be necessary for Governments concerned to reappoint delegations. Committee
                    will then convene to elect a chairman and to validate all actions taken since
                    December 31 last.<anchor n="15" xml:id="d450-f4"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d450-f1" n="12" xml:id="d450-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram No. 3142, June 5, not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d450-f2" n="13" xml:id="d450-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1942v05">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">v</hi></ref>,
                    section under Bolivia entitled “Negotiation for the Purchase by the United
                    States of Strategic Materials from Bolivia.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d450-f3" n="14" xml:id="d450-f3-content">
                <p>Signed for the Governments of Belgium, Bolivia, the United Kingdom, and the
                    Netherlands, September 9, 1942; for text, see League of Nations Treaty Series,
                    vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ccv</hi>, p. 137, or British Cmd. 6396, Treaty
                    Series No. 9 (1942).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d450-f4" n="15" xml:id="d450-f4-content">
                <p>The new Committee met for the first time on September 28, 1942.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
